Terraform Modules

Overview

This project demonstrates how to use Terraform modules to create EC2 instances with reusable configurations.

Two separate modules are created:

Linux module – creates an Amazon Linux EC2 instance and installs Apache (httpd).
Ubuntu module – creates an Ubuntu EC2 instance and installs Apache (apache2).

A single Security Group is created in the root module and shared by both EC2 modules.

Architecture

Root Module
│
├── Security Group
│      │
│      ├──────────────┐
│      ▼              ▼
│  Linux Module   Ubuntu Module
│      │              │
│      ▼              ▼
│ Amazon Linux      Ubuntu
│      EC2            EC2
│
└── Outputs
       │
       ├── Linux Instance ID
       └── Ubuntu Instance ID

Project Structure
.
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
├── outputs.tf
├── provider.tf
│
└── modules/
    ├── linux/
    │   ├── main.tf
    │   ├── variables.tf
    │   └── outputs.tf
    │
    └── ubuntu/
        ├── main.tf
        ├── variables.tf
        └── outputs.tf


Key Concepts

Creating reusable Terraform modules
Passing values from root module to child modules
Sharing a Security Group between multiple modules
Using module variables
Exposing resource attributes through module outputs
OS-specific EC2 bootstrapping using user_data
Module Input

The root module passes the Security Group ID to both modules:

security_group_id = aws_security_group.web_sg.id

Inside each module:

vpc_security_group_ids = [var.security_group_id]

Module Output

Each module exposes the EC2 instance ID:

output "instance_public_ip" {
  value       = aws_instance.ec2_instance.public_ip
  description = "The public IP address of the Ubuntu server"
}

The root module accesses it using:

output "linux_public_ip" {
  value = module.ec2_module_linux.instance_public_ip
}

Key Learning

Child modules encapsulate resources. Variables pass values into modules, while outputs expose selected values from modules to the parent.